  "query_preview": "..A shortwave trough continues to eject across\nnorthern Utah this evening, bringing continued showers and\nthunderstorms to the area. Isolated to scattered nocturnal \nconvection is expected to linger overnight. Runoff from earlier\nheavy rain will continue to cause rivers and streams to run high\nthrough much of the night, but the threat of additional flooding\nhas decreased significantly. \n\nOn Tuesday afternoon, another, weaker, shortwave trough will \neject across Utah. This will bring another round of showers and \nthunderstorms to the area. With weaker dynamics and decreasing \nPWATs, convection will not be as widespread compared to today. And\nwhile the threat of flash flooding will exist, it will also be \nless compared to today. There is currently a Marginal Risk of \nexcessive rainfall for southern, eastern, and portions of central \nUtah for Tuesday. \n\nWednesday through Friday should feature a bit of a lull in the\nweather as the airmass continues to trend drier. However, with\nenhanced southwest winds owing to a mean trough over the PacNW,\ndepending on how quickly the airmass and fuels dry out, focus may\nshift to fire weather concerns for midweek to late week \ntimeframe as fire weather conditions become increasingly elevated.\n\nFor the holiday weekend, models indicate some moisture being drawn\ninto the area from a tropical system over the eastern Pacific.\nHowever, there remains some uncertainty as to how much of this\nmoisture will reach the forecast area.",
